.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
c0
- C parser


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
c0 [options] file.x ast.1 data.2


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
C0
is the second pass of the C compiler.  It reads the lexeme stream
.bd
pass0
wrote and parses it, and writes two files:

.in +5
.ti -5
1.	The abstract syntax tree, one tree per function, in the file
named second.  This is what
.bd
c1
walks to generate code.

.ti -5
2.	The initialised data - string literals, initialisers,
everything whose value is known before the program runs - as
assembler, in the file named third.
.in -5

The split is what lets the code generator be a tree walk and nothing
else.  Data that has a value at compile time never reaches it.

.bd
C0
does the whole of the front end above lexing: scopes, declarations,
types, the usual arithmetic conversions, constant folding, and the
promotion and truncation the target's widths call for.  A tree
reaching
.bd
c1
has been type-checked and every conversion in it is explicit.

The three file arguments are positional and all three are required.
The driver names them for the source with the suffixes changed, and
removes them afterwards unless -k was given.

The options are:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-v mask	Set the debugging verbosity to the hexadecimal
.bd
mask.
Present only in a compiler built with debugging; the masks are listed
if the pass is run with no arguments.

.in -10
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1), pass0 (1), c1 (1), astpp (1)


.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
Errors name the file and line they were found on, taken from the line
markers in the lexeme stream, and are written to standard error.  The
exit status is non-zero if any were reported, and the output files
should not be used.
